---
/**
 * 06 ABOUT — the working background, then what the published work is.
 *
 * Every block here carries the locators it came from, and none of them prints
 * one. The prototype ended this section with a 未提供 block that named
 * 経歴 / 稼働条件 / 料金 and set each of them to 未記入; that block is gone
 * (IMPLEMENT-03 §5) and nothing replaced it.
 *
 * The reason is not that the gap is embarrassing. It is that a public page and
 * a prototype have different readers. In the prototype 未記入 was a note to the
 * people building it — a slot with a name and no value yet. To a visitor the
 * same three rows read as a statement the site is making about itself, and it
 * is a statement no source backs. Facts that arrive later get blocks here with
 * their sources filled in, which is the only way anything gets one.
 *
 * PROFILE_INVENTED = NO, still. #32 added the first profile fact this section
 * has ever carried and it is not invented: the owner decided what it says
 * (HD-C, Issue #32 comment 5748116465) and approved its wording (comment
 * 5748161578), and it is registered as a `user-fact` — the classification that
 * refuses a plausible-looking placeholder.
 *
 * WHAT #32 CHANGED IS THE READING ORDER. ABOUT used to be `now` followed by
 * three rows — 実装形態 / 公開範囲 / データ — and all three said the same kind
 * of thing: what this site is NOT claiming. A reader met three caveats in a row
 * and never met the person, because there was no line about the owner's working
 * background anywhere on the site. It now reads:
 *
 *   h2 → 現在の開発姿勢 (`now`) → 業務経験 → 掲載内容の公開境界
 *
 * The boundary is not softened, only moved and compressed: 合成データ is still
 * stated, and 掲載内容について now says what #29 established — a collaborative
 * project rebuilt for publication, personal technical demos, a self-directed
 * PoC — where 実装形態 called the whole portfolio 個人開発, which stopped being
 * true the moment #29 recorded the collaborative half.
 *
 * NO SENTENCE IS WRITTEN HERE. `site.about` holds ids and sourcing; the three
 * bodies live in the shipping registry and the three labels in the ui registry,
 * where their approval records are. That is the shape `site.howIBuild.premises`
 * took in #31, for the same reason — an approved string sitting in a component
 * is one `approvedCopyGate` cannot see, and both resolvers throw on an id their
 * registry does not hold, so a block pointing at nothing fails the build
 * instead of rendering an empty row.
 */
import { copyText } from '../../lib/content/copy.ts';
import { sectionNumber } from '../../lib/content/derive.ts';
import { site } from '../../lib/content/site.ts';
import { uiText } from '../../lib/content/ui.ts';
import Rail from '../layout/Rail.astro';

const a = site.about;
const index = sectionNumber('about');
const h2 = await copyText('home.about.h2');

/** One block, resolved: the label from ui, the body from the copy registry. */
const resolve = async (block: { id: string; labelId: string; copyId: string }) => ({
  id: block.id,
  label: uiText(block.labelId),
  body: await copyText(block.copyId),
});

const profile = await Promise.all(a.profile.map(resolve));
const disclosure = await Promise.all(a.disclosure.map(resolve));
---

{/* `data-mx-n` — see Hero.astro. The index is `site.about`'s own. */}
<section class="s-open" id="about" data-mx-n={index}>
  <div class="page">
    <div class="tr">
      <Rail index={index} labels={a.railLabels} />
      <div class="fld">
        <h2 class="ab-h">{h2}</h2>
        {/* #7 — how this engineer works, before anything else. ABOUT used to
            open with its own fine print, which told a reader who had just
            finished the work what the site was NOT claiming before telling
            them anything about the person. */}
        <div class="ab-now">
          {a.now.map((line) => <p>{line}</p>)}
        </div>
        {/* #32 — 業務経験. THE SAME `.spx` SHAPE AS EVERY OTHER FACT ON THIS
            SITE, deliberately: it is a profile fact, so it is drawn like one.
            A block of its own with a warning tone would make the one line about
            the person read as a fourth caveat, which is the structure this
            change exists to undo. Below 1024px — and, per motion.css, on
            desktop too — `.spx.wide .r` puts the mono label above the sentence,
            so the reading order is label then body at every width and the DOM
            order is that order. */}
        <div class="spx wide" style="margin-top:30px">
          {
            profile.map((block) => (
              <div class="r" data-about-fact={block.id}>
                <span class="k">{block.label}</span>
                {/* #7 — the locator is NOT rendered. `sourceRefs` stays
                    required on the record and stays under the Truth Gate — the
                    fact still has to say where it came from, the page just
                    stops printing its filing reference (spec §20). */}
                <span class="v">{block.body}</span>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        {/* #32 — 掲載内容について / 公開データ, in ONE container.
            The two are the compliance half and they are read together, so they
            share a block instead of standing as separate rows among the facts.
            `.ab-disc` is the whole of the difference: a hairline instead of the
            2px rule, and the supporting text colour at the supporting size. No
            alert, no banner, no `--stop` — these state what the published work
            IS, and colouring a fact as a warning is how a reader learns to
            discount it. */}
        <div class="spx wide ab-disc">
          {
            disclosure.map((block) => (
              <div class="r" data-about-disclosure={block.id}>
                <span class="k">{block.label}</span>
                <span class="v">{block.body}</span>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
